# SHA-256 checksums for `gif-python fetch-mindboggle`.
#
# Section 1 - the five volume archives of the Mindboggle-101 data set at Harvard Dataverse
# (doi:10.7910/DVN/HMQKCK; https://dataverse.harvard.edu/api/access/datafile/<id>). Computed on 2026-10-26
# from a complete download of every archive; the MD5 sums of the same files match those published by Dataverse
# for them (OASIS-TRT-20 2ef8b3fc..., NKI-TRT-20 b91e2630..., NKI-RS-22 6fc57e7e..., MMRR-21 867926c0...,
# Extra-18 14ab5236...). These lines are in `sha256sum` format:
#     sha256sum -c --ignore-missing checksums.sha256     (run in the folder that holds the archives)
#
# Section 2 - `cleaned-labels/<subject>/labels.DKT31.manual+aseg_cleaned.nii.gz`: the labels after
# `gif-python clean-labels` with the default labels (1001 1032 1033 2001 2032 2033), for all 101 subjects.
# The value is NOT the hash of the file: it is the SHA-256 of the data type, the shape and the voxel values
# (C order, little endian) of the volume, so that it does not depend on the nibabel or zlib version that wrote
# the file (see src/datasets/mindboggle.py, sha256_label_volume). `fetch-mindboggle --clean` checks it.
#
# Do not edit by hand; regenerate with `gif-python fetch-mindboggle --clean --write-checksums FILE`.
